<p>Roque Nublo is not a minor viewpoint dressed up for tourism — it is a genuine volcanic monolith rising roughly 80 metres from its base, with the surrounding summit area sitting at around 1,813 metres above sea level, which makes it the third-highest point on Gran Canaria. Formed from compacted volcanic ash and basalt left standing after softer surrounding rock eroded away over millions of years, the rock has become one of the most recognisable natural symbols of the island, appearing on local emblems, tourism branding, and in countless photographs taken by residents and visitors alike. In recognition of its natural and cultural value, Roque Nublo was declared a protected natural monument in 1987, and it now sits within the wider Roque Nublo Rural Park, a protected landscape designated in 1994 that covers much of the surrounding highland terrain. Reaching the base of the monolith itself involves a walk along a marked trail from the nearest parking area, and your chauffeur can wait at the trailhead while you make that walk up and back at whatever pace suits your group.</p>
